# XAMPP

**XAMPP** is a local web development environment.

It provides several components needed to develop and test PHP applications locally.

XAMPP includes:

```text
Apache
MySQL
PHP
Perl
```

XAMPP allows developers to create a local environment where they can:

* Write PHP applications
* Run applications
* Test applications
* Work with databases
* Develop websites without publishing them online

---

# WAMP, LAMP, MAMP and XAMPP

There are different development environments.

## WAMP

**WAMP** stands for:

```text
Windows
Apache
MySQL
PHP
```

It is designed for Windows.

## LAMP

**LAMP** represents:

```text
Linux
Apache
MySQL
PHP
```

## MAMP

**MAMP** represents:

```text
Macintosh
Apache
MySQL
PHP
```

## XAMPP

XAMPP is cross-platform and can work on:

* Windows
* Linux
* macOS

The course recommends XAMPP for setting up a development server.

---

# Apache Web Server

Apache is a web server used to serve web pages and web applications.

The **Apache HTTP Server** is:

* Free
* Open source
* Cross-platform

Other popular web servers include:

* Apache
* Nginx
* Microsoft IIS

---

# Starting XAMPP

When developing a PHP application, Apache needs to be running.

If your application uses a database, MySQL should also be started.

Typical XAMPP setup:

```text
Apache     → Start
MySQL      → Start
```

Then your PHP application can be accessed through the browser.

---

# Localhost

**localhost** refers to the local computer being used as a server.

Instead of accessing a website on the Internet, you can run your PHP application on your own computer.

Common localhost addresses are:

```text
http://localhost
```

or:

```text
http://127.0.0.1
```

These addresses point to the local machine.

---

# Apache Default Port

The default Apache port in XAMPP is:

```text
80
```

Therefore, you can normally access your local server using:

```text
http://localhost
```

However, another application may already be using port 80.

If port 80 is busy, Apache may not start correctly.

---

# Changing the Apache Port

If port 80 is already being used, Apache can be configured to use another port, such as:

```text
8080
```

Then the application can be accessed using:

```text
http://localhost:8080
```

For example:

```text
http://localhost:8080/example.php
```

The port number is written after a colon:

```text
localhost:8080
```

---

# Document Root

The **document root** is the main directory from which the local web server serves files.

For XAMPP on Windows, the default document root is:

```text
C:\xampp\htdocs
```

All local web projects are normally placed inside the `htdocs` directory.

For example:

```text
C:\xampp\htdocs\myproject
```

Then the project can be accessed through:

```text
http://localhost/myproject/
```

---

# Project Structure Example

A simple PHP project can look like this:

```text
htdocs/
│
└── myproject/
    │
    ├── index.php
    ├── about.php
    ├── contact.php
    └── style.css
```

The `htdocs` directory is not included in the browser URL because it is configured as the web server's document root.

---

# Creating the First PHP Page

Create a project inside:

```text
C:\xampp\htdocs
```

For example:

```text
C:\xampp\htdocs\test
```

Then create:

```text
index.php
```

Inside the file:

```php
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Home Page</title>
</head>

<body>

    <h1>Hello World!</h1>

    <?php
        echo "Welcome to PHP & MYSQL Course";
    ?>

</body>

</html>
```

---

# PHP Syntax

PHP code is placed between PHP opening and closing tags:

```php
<?php

// PHP code goes here

?>
```

For example:

```php
<?php
echo "Hello World";
?>
```

The `echo` statement is used to output content.

---

# Accessing the PHP Page

If the project is:

```text
C:\xampp\htdocs\test
```

and the file is:

```text
index.php
```

you can open it in the browser using:

```text
http://localhost/test/
```

If Apache uses port 8080:

```text
http://localhost:8080/test/
```

---

# Index Page

An `index.php` file is commonly used as the default page of a website or project.

For example:

```text
http://localhost/test/
```

can automatically load:

```text
index.php
```

Therefore, it is not necessary to write:

```text
http://localhost/test/index.php
```

The server can automatically load the index page.

